% lps-converted-from: fox_crow.lps
% This document was written from the LPS program fox_crow.lps, which says the same thing in the older syntax. The two run alike, and tools/lps_to_le_test.pl checks that they do. The first line above is what keeps the older file from being read as this document's companion, which would run every rule twice.

the target language is: lps.

the maximum time is 10.

the events are:
    *a thing* sees *a second thing*.

the actions are:
    *a thing* praises *a second thing*; known as praise.
    *a thing* picks up *a second thing*; known as pick_up.
    *a thing* drops *a second thing*; known as drop.
    *a thing* does sing; known as sing.

the fluents are:
    *a thing* has *a second thing*.
    *a thing* is near *a second thing*; known as near.
    *a thing* sings.
    *a thing* is fooled; known as fooled.

the knowledge base fox_crow includes:

initially crow has cheese
    and fox is near crow.

when a thing praises a second thing
then the second thing sings.

when a thing does sing
then the thing sings.

when a thing drops a second thing
then it is not the case that the thing has the second thing.

when a thing drops a second thing
then ground is near the second thing.

when a thing picks up a second thing
then the thing has the second thing.

when a thing picks up a second thing
then it is not the case that ground is near the second thing.

if crow has cheese at a time
    and fox is near crow at the time
    and it is not the case that fox has cheese at the time
then fox praises crow from the time
    and ground is near cheese at a second time
    and fox picks up cheese from the second time.

if a thing sings
    and the thing has a second thing
then the thing drops the second thing.

when crow sees a thing
then crow is fooled.

it must not be true that
    crow does sing
    and crow is fooled.

